  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence is $f(n)=5n-4$ for $n\\ge 1.$  What is the recursive formula for this sequence?",
  "worked_solution": "The recursive formula for an arithmetic sequence (in function notation) is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)+d,\\,f(1)=___\\,,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo complete the formula, we need to work out the first term and common difference. The first two terms are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =5(1)-4=1 \\\\ f(2) & =5(2)-4=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =f(2)-f(1) \\\\  & =6-1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first term is $f(1)=1$ and the common difference is $d=5.$ Therefore, the recursive formula is as follows:\n\n$$\nf(n+1)=f(n)+5,\\,f(1)=1,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$",
